---
description: >
  Alignment drift after the cold-start install refactor: stale scope path and
  identity-locked references to "aide_init" as the sole caller.
intent: >
  configureIde's spec scope still names the old path and the intent
  paragraph names "aide_init" as the helper's caller. With the install
  service now consumed by both the MCP tool and the CLI, identity-locked
  caller references at the leaf undermine the multi-consumer story the
  parent service must adopt.
misalignment:
  - spec-gap
---

## Issues

- [ ] `src/service/install/configureIde/.aide` frontmatter
  `scope: src/tools/init/configureIde`
  Stale path — module moved to `src/service/install/configureIde/`. The
  scope field is a positional commitment that no longer matches reality.
  Traces to: physical move (git rename
  `src/tools/init/configureIde/.aide -> src/service/install/configureIde/.aide`);
  ancestor `.aide/intent.aide` -> outcomes.desired[4] (folder tree as
  architecture).
  Misalignment: spec-gap

- [ ] `src/service/install/configureIde/.aide` intent paragraph:
  "configureIde is the execution site for AIDE's IDE-integration strategy:
  the helpers **aide_init calls** to make `.aide` files render as Markdown
  in every supported editor."
  Identity drift — names `aide_init` as the caller, but the install
  service is now also called by the CLI. The helper itself is
  caller-agnostic; the spec should describe it as a service helper of
  the install module, not of the MCP tool.
  Traces to: parent `src/service/install/.aide` (currently misaligned —
  see its todo for the install-service identity issue); ancestor
  `src/.aide` -> outcomes.desired[3] (client-specific logic confined to
  aide_init) — this caller-agnostic helper should be unaffected by which
  consumer triggers it.
  Misalignment: spec-gap

## Retro

Same root cause as the parent: a folder-rename plus a new consumer should
have triggered a `/aide:spec` pass on every node in the moved subtree.
The "aide_init calls" phrase in the intent paragraph is a small textual
edit but a load-bearing identity claim — every caller-agnostic helper
under the install service needs the same edit.
